Number of Islands (Time Comp = O(m × n) Space Comp = O(m × n))

class Solution {
    private void dfs(int row,int col, char[][] grid){

        int m=grid.length;
        int n=grid[0].length;

        if(row<0 || col<0 || row>=m || col>=n || grid[row][col]=='0'){
            return;
        }

        grid[row][col]='0';

        dfs(row-1,col,grid);//up
        dfs(row+1,col,grid);//down
        dfs(row,col-1,grid);//left
        dfs(row,col+1,grid);//right

    }


    public int numIslands(char[][] grid) {

        int m=grid.length;
        int n=grid[0].length;

        int count=0;

        for(int i=0;i<m;i++){
            for(int j=0;j<n;j++){
                if(grid[i][j]=='1'){
                    count++;
                    dfs(i,j,grid);
                }
            }
        }
        return count;
    }
}



### Number of Islands — DFS Algorithm

1. Initialize `count = 0` to track the number of islands.
2. Traverse every cell in the grid using nested loops.
3. If `grid[i][j] == '1'`, a new island is found; increment `count`.
4. Call DFS from that cell to explore all connected land cells.
5. In DFS, check boundaries and return if the cell is out of bounds or contains water (`'0'`).
6. Mark the current land cell as visited by changing `'1'` to `'0'`.
7. Recursively explore all four directions: **up, down, left, right**.
8. Continue scanning the grid; skip water and already visited cells.
9. Return `count` after traversing the entire grid.

**Time Complexity:** `O(m × n)`  
**Space Complexity:** `O(m × n)` worst case due to the DFS recursion stack.
